## an4.179
an4.179:1.1 | S: Then Venerable Ānanda went up to Venerable Sāriputta, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Ven. Ānanda went to Ven. Sāriputta and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
an4.179:1.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, Ānanda sat down to one side, and said to Sāriputta: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to Ven. Sāriputta,
an4.179:1.3 | S: “What is the cause, Reverend Sāriputta, what is the reason why some sentient beings are not fully extinguished in this very life?” | T: “Friend Sāriputta, what is the cause, what is the reason, why some beings do not become totally unbound in the present life?”
an4.179:2.1 | S: “Reverend Ānanda, it’s because some sentient beings don’t really understand which perceptions make things worse, which keep things steady, which lead to distinction, and which lead to penetration. | T: “There’s the case, friend Ānanda, where beings do not discern, as it has come to be, that ‘This perception has a share in decline;’ ‘This perception has a share in stability;’ ‘This perception has a share in distinction;’ ‘This perception has a share in penetration.’
an4.179:2.2 | S: That’s the cause, that’s the reason why some sentient beings are not fully extinguished in this very life.” | T: This is the cause, this is the reason, why some beings do not become totally unbound in the present life.”
an4.179:3.1 | S: “What is the cause, Reverend Sāriputta, what is the reason why some sentient beings are fully extinguished in this very life?” | T: “And what, friend Sāriputta, is the cause, what is the reason, why some beings do become totally unbound in the present life?”
an4.179:3.2 | S: “Reverend Ānanda, it’s because some sentient beings truly understand which perceptions make things worse, which keep things steady, which lead to distinction, and which lead to penetration. | T: “There’s the case, friend Ānanda, where beings discern, as it has come to be, that ‘This perception has a share in decline;’ ‘This perception has a share in stability;’ ‘This perception has a share in distinction;’ ‘This perception has a share in penetration.’
an4.179:3.3 | S: That’s the cause, that’s the reason why some sentient beings are fully extinguished in this very life.” | T: This is the cause, this is the reason, why some beings become totally unbound in the present life.”
an4.179:3.4 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.181
an4.181:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, a warrior with four factors is worthy of a king, fit to serve a king, and is reckoned a factor of kingship. | T: “Endowed with four qualities, monks, a warrior is worthy of a king, an asset to a king, and counts as a very limb of his king.
an4.181:1.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.181:1.3 | S: He’s skilled in the basics, a long-distance shooter, a marksman, one who shatters large objects. | T: “There is the case where a warrior is skilled in his stance, able to shoot far, able to fire shots in rapid succession, and able to pierce great objects.
an4.181:1.4 | S: A warrior with these four factors is worthy of a king, fit to serve a king, and is reckoned a factor of kingship. | T: A warrior endowed with these four qualities is worthy of a king, an asset to a king, and counts as a very limb of his king.
an4.181:1.5 | S: In the same way, a mendicant with four qualities is worthy of offerings dedicated to the gods, worthy of hospitality, worthy of a religious donation, worthy of veneration with cupped palms, and is the supreme field of merit for the world. | T: “In the same way a monk endowed with four qualities is deserving of gifts, deserving of hospitality, deserving of offerings, deserving of respect, an unexcelled field of merit for the world.
an4.181:1.6 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.181:1.7 | S: He’s skilled in the basics, a long-distance shooter, a marksman, one who shatters large objects. | T: “There is the case where a monk is skilled in his stance, able to shoot far, able to fire shots in rapid succession, and able to pierce great objects. A monk endowed with these four qualities is deserving of gifts, deserving of hospitality, deserving of offerings, deserving of respect, an unexcelled field of merit for the world.
an4.181:2.1 | S: And how is a mendicant skilled in the basics? | T: “And how is a monk skilled in his stance?
an4.181:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant is ethical, restrained in the monastic code, conducting themselves well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, they keep the rules they’ve undertaken. | T: There is the case where a monk is virtuous. He dwells restrained in accordance with the Pāṭimokkha, consummate in his behavior & sphere of activity. He trains himself, having undertaken the training rules, seeing danger in the slightest faults.
an4.181:2.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant is skilled in the basics. | T: This is how a monk is skilled in his stance.
an4.181:3.1 | S: And how is a mendicant a long-distance shooter? | T: “And how is a monk one who is able to shoot far?
an4.181:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant truly sees any kind of form at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> form—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: There is the case where a monk sees any form whatsoever that is past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle; common or sublime; far or near—every form—as it has come to be with right discernment as: ‘This is not mine. This is not my self. This is not what I am.’
an4.181:3.3 | S: They truly see any kind of feeling … | T: “He sees any feeling whatsoever.…
an4.181:3.4 | S: perception … | T: “He sees any perception whatsoever.…
an4.181:3.5 | S: choices … | T: “He sees any fabrications whatsoever.…
an4.181:3.6 | S: consciousness at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> consciousness—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: “He sees any consciousness whatsoever that is past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle, common or sublime, far or near—every consciousness—as it has come to be with right discernment as: ‘This is not mine. This is not my self. This is not what I am.’
an4.181:3.7 | S: That’s how a mendicant is a long-distance shooter. | T: “This is how a monk is one who is able to shoot far.
an4.181:4.1 | S: And how is a mendicant a marksman? | T: “And how is a monk one who is able to fire shots in rapid succession?
an4.181:4.2 | S: It’s when they truly understand: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’. | T: There is the case where a monk discerns, as it has come to be, that ‘This is stress’ … ‘This is the origination of stress’ … ‘This is the cessation of stress’ … ‘This is the path of practice leading to the cessation of stress.’
an4.181:4.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant is a marksman. | T: This is how a monk is one who is able to fire shots in rapid succession.
an4.181:5.1 | S: And how does a mendicant shatter large objects? | T: “And how is a monk one who is able to pierce great objects?
an4.181:5.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant shatters the great mass of ignorance. | T: There is the case where a monk pierces right through the great mass of ignorance.
an4.181:5.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant shatters large objects. | T: This is how a monk is one who is able to pierce great objects right through.
an4.181:5.4 | S: A mendicant with these four qualities … is the supreme field of merit for the world.” | T: “Endowed with these four qualities, a monk is deserving of gifts, deserving of hospitality, deserving of offerings, deserving of respect, an unexcelled field of merit for the world.”
an4.181:5.5 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.182
an4.182:1.1 | S: “There are four things that no one can guarantee— | T: “For four things, monks, there is no guarantor—
an4.182:1.2 | S: not an ascetic, a brahmin, a god, a Māra, a divinity, or anyone in the world. | T: neither a contemplative, nor a brahman, nor a deva, nor a Māra, nor a Brahmā, nor anyone at all in the world.
an4.182:2.1 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.182:2.2 | S: No one can guarantee that someone liable to old age will not grow old. | T: “‘May what is subject to aging not age’: For this there is no guarantor—
an4.182:2.3 | P: samaṇo vā brāhmaṇo vā devo vā māro vā brahmā vā koci vā lokasmiṁ; | T: neither a contemplative, nor a brahman, nor a deva, nor a Māra, nor a Brahmā, nor anyone at all in the world.
an4.182:2.4 | S: No one can guarantee that someone liable to sickness will not get sick. | T: “‘May what is subject to illness not fall ill’: For this there is no guarantor—
an4.182:2.5 | P: samaṇo vā brāhmaṇo vā devo vā māro vā brahmā vā koci vā lokasmiṁ; | T: neither a contemplative, nor a brahman, nor a deva, nor a Māra, nor a Brahmā, nor anyone at all in the world.
an4.182:2.6 | S: No one can guarantee that someone liable to death will not die. | T: “‘May what is subject to death not die’: For this there is no guarantor—
an4.182:2.7 | P: samaṇo vā brāhmaṇo vā devo vā māro vā brahmā vā koci vā lokasmiṁ; | T: neither a contemplative, nor a brahman, nor a deva, nor a Māra, nor a Brahmā, nor anyone at all in the world.
an4.182:2.8 | S: No one can guarantee that the bad deeds done in past lives—corrupting, leading to future lives, hurtful, resulting in suffering and future rebirth, old age, and death—will not produce their result. | T: “‘May the results of evil actions—defiled, leading to further becoming, distressing, ripening in pain & future birth, aging, & death—not appear’: For this there is no guarantor—
an4.182:2.9 | P: samaṇo vā brāhmaṇo vā devo vā māro vā brahmā vā koci vā lokasmiṁ. | T: neither a contemplative, nor a brahman, nor a deva, nor a Māra, nor a Brahmā, nor anyone at all in the world.
an4.182:3.1 | S: These are the four things that no one can guarantee— | T: “For these four things, monks, there is no guarantor—
an4.182:3.2 | S: not an ascetic, a brahmin, a god, a Māra, a divinity, or anyone in the world.” | T: neither a contemplative, nor a brahman, nor a deva, nor a Māra, nor a Brahmā, nor anyone at all in the world.”
an4.182:3.3 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.192
an4.192:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, these four things can be known in four situations. | T: “Monks, these four traits may be known by means of four (other) traits.
an4.192:1.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.192:1.3 | S: You can get to know a person’s ethics by living with them. But only after a long time, not casually; only when attentive, not when inattentive; and only by the wise, not the witless. | T: “It’s through living together that a person’s virtue may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning.
an4.192:1.4 | S: You can get to know a person’s purity by dealing with them. … | T: “It’s through dealing with a person that his purity may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning.
an4.192:1.5 | S: You can get to know a person’s resilience in times of trouble. … | T: “It’s through adversity that a person’s endurance may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning.
an4.192:1.6 | S: You can get to know a person’s wisdom by discussion. But only after a long time, not casually; only when attentive, not when inattentive; and only by the wise, not the witless. | T: “It’s through discussion that a person’s discernment may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning.
an4.192:2.1 | S: ‘You can get to know a person’s ethics by living with them. But only after a long time, not casually; only when attentive, not when inattentive; and only by the wise, not the witless.’ | T: [1] “‘It’s through living together that a person’s virtue may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning’:
an4.192:2.2 | S: That’s what I said, but why did I say it? | T: Thus it was said. And in reference to what was it said?
an4.192:2.3 | S: Take an individual who’s living with someone else. They come to know: | T: “There is the case where one individual, through living with another, knows this:
an4.192:2.4 | S: ‘For a long time this venerable’s deeds have been broken, tainted, spotty, and marred. Their deeds and behavior are inconsistent. | T: ‘For a long time this person has been torn, broken, spotted, splattered in his actions. He hasn’t been consistent in his actions. He hasn’t practiced consistently with regard to the precepts.
an4.192:2.5 | S: This venerable is unethical, not ethical.’ | T: He is an unprincipled person, not a virtuous, principled one.’
an4.192:3.1 | S: Take another individual who’s living with someone else. They come to know: | T: And then there is the case where one individual, through living with another, knows this:
an4.192:3.2 | S: ‘For a long time this venerable’s deeds have been intact, impeccable, spotless, and unmarred. Their deeds and behavior are consistent. | T: ‘For a long time this person has been untorn, unbroken, unspotted, unsplattered in his actions. He has been consistent in his actions. He has practiced consistently with regard to the precepts.
an4.192:3.3 | S: This venerable is ethical, not unethical.’ | T: He is a virtuous, principled person, not an unprincipled one.’
an4.192:3.4 | S: That’s why I said that you can get to know a person’s ethics by living with them. But only after a long time, not a short time; only when attentive, not when inattentive; and only by the wise, not the witless. | T: “‘It’s through living together that a person’s virtue may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning’: Thus it was said. And in reference to this was it said.
an4.192:4.1 | S: ‘You can get to know a person’s purity by dealing with them. …’ | T: [2] “‘It’s through dealing with a person that his purity may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning’:
an4.192:4.2 | S: That’s what I said, but why did I say it? | T: Thus it was said. And in reference to what was it said?
an4.192:4.3 | S: Take an individual who has dealings with someone else. They come to know: | T: “There is the case where one individual, through dealing with another, knows this:
an4.192:4.4 | S: ‘This venerable deals with one person in one way. Then they deal with two, three, or many people each in different ways. | T: ‘This person deals one way when one-on-one, another way when with two, another way when with three, another way when with many.
an4.192:4.5 | S: They’re not consistent from one deal to the next. | T: His earlier dealings do not jibe with his later dealings.
an4.192:4.6 | S: This venerable’s dealings are impure, not pure.’ | T: He is impure in his dealings, not pure.’
an4.192:5.1 | S: Take another individual who has dealings with someone else. They come to know: | T: And then there is the case where one individual, through dealing with another, knows this:
an4.192:5.2 | S: ‘This venerable deals with one person in one way. Then they deal with two, three, or many people each in the same way. | T: ‘The way this person deals when one-on-one, is the same way he deals when with two, when with three, when with many.
an4.192:5.3 | S: They’re consistent from one deal to the next. | T: His earlier dealings jibe with his later dealings.
an4.192:5.4 | S: This venerable’s dealings are pure, not impure.’ | T: He is pure in his dealings, not impure.’
an4.192:5.5 | S: That’s why I said that you can get to know a person’s purity by dealing with them. … | T: “‘It’s through dealing with a person that his purity may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning’: Thus it was said. And in reference to this was it said.
an4.192:6.1 | S: ‘You can get to know a person’s resilience in times of trouble. …’ | T: [3] “‘It’s through adversity that a person’s endurance may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning’:
an4.192:6.2 | S: That’s what I said, but why did I say it? | T: Thus it was said. And in reference to what was it said?
an4.192:6.3 | S: Take someone who experiences loss of family, wealth, or health. But they don’t reflect: | T: “There is the case where a person, suffering loss of relatives, loss of wealth, or loss through disease, doesn’t reflect:
an4.192:6.4 | S: ‘Worldly life is like that. Incarnation is like that. That’s why the eight worldly conditions revolve around the world, and the world revolves around the eight worldly conditions: | T: ‘That’s how it is when living together in the world. That’s how it is when gaining a personal identity [atta-bhāva, literally “self-state”]. When there is living in the world, when there is the gaining of a personal identity, these eight worldly conditions spin after the world, and the world spins after these eight worldly conditions:
an4.192:6.5 | S: gain and loss, fame and disgrace, blame and praise, pleasure and pain.’ | T: gain, loss, status, disgrace, censure, praise, pleasure, & pain.’
an4.192:6.6 | S: They sorrow and wail and lament, beating their breast and falling into confusion. | T: Suffering loss of relatives, loss of wealth, or loss through disease, he sorrows, grieves, & laments, beats his breast, becomes distraught.
an4.192:7.1 | S: Take someone else who experiences loss of family, wealth, or health. But they reflect: | T: And then there is the case where a person, suffering loss of relatives, loss of wealth, or loss through disease, reflects:
an4.192:7.2 | S: ‘Worldly life is like that. Incarnation is like that. That’s why the eight worldly conditions revolve around the world, and the world revolves around the eight worldly conditions: | T: ‘That’s how it is when living together in the world. That’s how it is when gaining a personal identity. When there is living in the world, when there is the gaining of a personal identity, these eight worldly conditions spin after the world, and the world spins after these eight worldly conditions:
an4.192:7.3 | S: gain and loss, fame and disgrace, blame and praise, pleasure and pain.’ | T: gain, loss, status, disgrace, censure, praise, pleasure, & pain.’
an4.192:7.4 | S: They don’t sorrow or wail or lament, beating their breast and falling into confusion. | T: Suffering loss of relatives, loss of wealth, or loss through disease, he doesn’t sorrow, grieve, or lament, doesn’t beat his breast or become distraught.
an4.192:7.5 | S: That’s why I said that you can get to know a person’s resilience in times of trouble. … | T: “‘It’s through adversity that a person’s endurance may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning’:
an4.192:7.6 | P: iti yaṁ taṁ vuttaṁ idametaṁ paṭicca vuttaṁ. | T: Thus it was said. And in reference to this was it said.
an4.192:8.1 | S: ‘You can get to know a person’s wisdom by discussion. But only after a long time, not casually; only when attentive, not when inattentive; and only by the wise, not the witless.’ | T: [4] “‘It’s through discussion that a person’s discernment may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning’:
an4.192:8.2 | S: That’s what I said, but why did I say it? | T: Thus it was said. And in reference to what was it said?
an4.192:8.3 | S: Take an individual who is discussing with someone else. They come to know: | T: “There is the case where one individual, through discussion with another, knows this:
an4.192:8.4 | S: ‘Judging by this venerable’s approach, by what they’re getting at, and by how they discuss a question, they’re witless, not wise. | T: ‘From the way this person rises to an issue, from the way he applies (his reasoning), from the way he addresses a question, he is dull, not discerning.
an4.192:8.5 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an4.192:8.6 | S: This venerable does not bring up a deep and meaningful saying that is peaceful, sublime, beyond the scope of logic, subtle, comprehensible to the astute. | T: He doesn’t make statements that are deep, tranquil, refined, beyond the scope of conjecture, subtle, to-be-experienced by the wise.
an4.192:8.7 | S: When this venerable speaks on Dhamma they’re not able to explain the meaning, either briefly or in detail. They can’t teach it, assert it, establish it, clarify it, analyze it, or reveal it. | T: He cannot declare the meaning, teach it, describe it, set it forth, reveal it, explain it, or make it plain.
an4.192:8.8 | S: This venerable is witless, not wise.’ | T: He is dull, not discerning.’
an4.192:9.1 | S: Suppose a person with clear eyes was standing on the bank of a lake. They’d see a little fish rising, | T: Just as if a man with good eyesight standing on the shore of a body of water were to see a small fish rise.
an4.192:9.2 | S: and think: | T: The thought would occur to him,
an4.192:9.3 | S: ‘Judging by this fish’s approach, by the ripples it makes, and by its force, it’s a little fish, not a big one.’ | T: ‘From the rise of this fish, from the break of its ripples, from its speed, it is a small fish, not a large one.’
an4.192:9.4 | S: In the same way, an individual who is discussing with someone else would come to know: | T: In the same way, one individual, in discussion with another, knows this:
an4.192:9.5 | S: ‘Judging by this venerable’s approach, by what they’re getting at, and by how they discuss a question, they’re witless, not wise. …’ | T: ‘From the way this person rises to an issue, from the way he applies (his reasoning), from the way he addresses a question…
an4.192:9.6 | P: Taṁ kissa hetu? | T: (none)
an4.192:9.7 | P: Tathā hi ayamāyasmā na ceva gambhīraṁ atthapadaṁ udāharati santaṁ paṇītaṁ atakkāvacaraṁ nipuṇaṁ paṇḍitavedanīyaṁ. | T: (none)
an4.192:9.8 | P: Yañca ayamāyasmā dhammaṁ bhāsati, tassa ca na paṭibalo saṅkhittena vā vitthārena vā atthaṁ ācikkhituṁ desetuṁ paññāpetuṁ paṭṭhapetuṁ vivarituṁ vibhajituṁ uttānīkātuṁ. | T: (none)
an4.192:9.9 | P: Duppañño ayamāyasmā, nāyamāyasmā paññavā’ti. | T: he is dull, not discerning.’
an4.192:10.1 | S: Take another individual who is discussing with someone else. They come to know: | T: “And then there is the case where one individual, through discussion with another, knows this:
an4.192:10.2 | S: ‘Judging by this venerable’s approach, by what they’re getting at, and by how they discuss a question, they’re wise, not witless. | T: ‘From the way this person rises to an issue, from the way he applies (his reasoning), from the way he addresses a question, he is discerning, not dull.
an4.192:10.3 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an4.192:10.4 | S: This venerable brings up a deep and meaningful saying that is peaceful, sublime, beyond the scope of logic, subtle, comprehensible to the astute. | T: He makes statements that are deep, tranquil, refined, beyond the scope of conjecture, subtle, to-be-experienced by the wise.
an4.192:10.5 | S: When this venerable speaks on Dhamma they’re able to explain the meaning, either briefly or in detail. They teach it, assert it, establish it, clarify it, analyze it, and reveal it. | T: He can declare the meaning, teach it, describe it, set it forth, reveal it, explain it, & make it plain.
an4.192:10.6 | S: This venerable is wise, not witless.’ | T: He is discerning, not dull.’
an4.192:11.1 | S: Suppose a man with clear eyes was standing on the bank of a lake. He’d see a big fish rising, | T: Just as if a man with good eyesight standing on the shore of a body of water were to see a large fish rise.
an4.192:11.2 | S: and think: | T: The thought would occur to him,
an4.192:11.3 | S: ‘Judging by this fish’s approach, by the ripples it makes, and by its force, it’s a big fish, not a little one.’ | T: ‘From the rise of this fish, from the break of its ripples, from its speed, it is a large fish, not a small one.’
an4.192:11.4 | S: In the same way, an individual who is discussing with someone else would come to know: | T: In the same way, one individual, in discussion with another, knows this:
an4.192:11.5 | S: ‘Judging by this venerable’s approach, by what they’re getting at, and by how they articulate a question, they’re wise, not witless. …’ | T: ‘From the way this person rises to an issue, from the way he applies (his reasoning), from the way he addresses a question… he is discerning, not dull.’
an4.192:11.6 | P: Taṁ kissa hetu? | T: (none)
an4.192:11.7 | P: Tathā hi ayamāyasmā gambhīrañceva atthapadaṁ udāharati santaṁ paṇītaṁ atakkāvacaraṁ nipuṇaṁ paṇḍitavedanīyaṁ. | T: (none)
an4.192:11.8 | P: Yañca ayamāyasmā dhammaṁ bhāsati, tassa ca paṭibalo saṅkhittena vā vitthārena vā atthaṁ ācikkhituṁ desetuṁ paññāpetuṁ paṭṭhapetuṁ vivarituṁ vibhajituṁ uttānīkātuṁ. | T: (none)
an4.192:11.9 | P: Paññavā ayamāyasmā, nāyamāyasmā duppañño’ti. | T: (none)
an4.192:12.1 | S: That’s why I said that you can get to know a person’s wisdom by discussion. But only after a long time, not casually; only when attentive, not when inattentive; and only by the wise, not the witless. | T: “‘It’s through discussion that a person’s discernment may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning’: Thus it was said. And in reference to this was it said.
an4.192:12.2 | S: These are the four things that can be known in four situations.” | T: “These, monks, are the four traits that may be known by means of these four (other) traits.”
an4.192:12.3 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.194
an4.194:1.1 | S: At one time Venerable Ānanda was staying in the land of the Koliyans, where they have a town named Sāpūga. | T: On one occasion Ven. Ānanda was staying among the Koliyans, near the Koliyan town named Sāpuga.
an4.194:1.2 | S: Then several Koliyans from Sāpūga went up to Ānanda, bowed, and sat down to one side. Then Venerable Ānanda said to them: | T: Then a large number of Koliyan-sons from Sāpuga went to him and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As they were sitting there Ven. Ānanda said to them,
an4.194:2.1 | S: “Byagghapajjas, these four factors of trying to be pure have been rightly explained by the Blessed One, who knows and sees, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha. They are in order to purify sentient beings, to get past sorrow and crying, to make an end of pain and sadness, to discover the system, and to realize extinguishment. | T: “TigerPaws, these four factors for exertion with regard to purity have been rightly expounded by the Blessed One who knows & sees—the Worthy One, the Rightly Self-awakened One—for the purification of beings, for the overcoming of sorrow & lamentation, for the disappearance of pain & distress, for the attainment of the right method, & for the realization of unbinding.
an4.194:2.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.194:2.3 | S: The factors of trying to be pure in ethics, mind, view, and freedom. | T: The factor for exertion with regard to purity of virtue, the factor for exertion with regard to purity of mind, the factor for exertion with regard to purity of view, and the factor for exertion with regard to purity of release.
an4.194:3.1 | S: And what is the factor of trying to be pure in ethics? | T: “And what, TigerPaws, is the factor for exertion with regard to purity of virtue?
an4.194:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant is ethical, restrained in the monastic code, conducting themselves well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, they keep the rules they’ve undertaken. | T: There is the case where a monk is virtuous. He dwells restrained in accordance with the Pāṭimokkha, consummate in his behavior & sphere of activity. He trains himself, having undertaken the training rules, seeing danger in the slightest faults.
an4.194:3.3 | S: This is called purity of ethics. | T: This is called purity of virtue.
an4.194:3.4 | S: They think: ‘I will fulfill such purity of ethics, or, if it’s already fulfilled, I’ll support it in every situation by wisdom.’ Their enthusiasm for that—their effort, zeal, vigor, perseverance, mindfulness, and situational awareness—is called the factor of trying to be pure in ethics. | T: (The thought,) ‘I will make complete this sort of purity of virtue when it is not yet complete, or I will protect it here & there with discernment when it is complete’: Any desire, effort, diligence, endeavor, relentlessness, mindfulness, & alertness there is called the factor for exertion with regard to purity of virtue.
an4.194:4.1 | S: And what is the factor of trying to be pure in mind? | T: “And what, TigerPaws, is the factor for exertion with regard to purity of mind?
an4.194:4.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant, quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, enters and remains in the first absorption … second absorption … third absorption … fourth absorption. | T: There is the case where a monk—quite secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities—enters & remains in the first jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation. With the stilling of directed thoughts & evaluations, he enters & remains in the second jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of concentration, unification of awareness free from directed thought & evaluation—internal assurance. With the fading of rapture, he remains equanimous, mindful, & alert, and senses pleasure with the body. He enters & remains in the third jhāna, of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous & mindful, he has a pleasant abiding.’ With the abandoning of pleasure & pain—as with the earlier disappearance of elation & distress—he enters & remains in the fourth jhāna: purity of equanimity & mindfulness, neither pleasure nor pain.
an4.194:4.3 | S: This is called purity of mind. | T: This is called purity of mind.
an4.194:4.4 | S: They think: ‘I will fulfill such purity of mind, or, if it’s already fulfilled, I’ll support it in every situation by wisdom.’ Their enthusiasm for that—their effort, zeal, vigor, perseverance, mindfulness, and situational awareness—is called the factor of trying to be pure in mind. | T: (The thought,) ‘I will make complete this sort of purity of mind when it is not yet complete, or I will protect it here & there with discernment when it is complete’: Any desire, effort, diligence, endeavor, relentlessness, mindfulness, & alertness there is called the factor for exertion with regard to purity of mind.
an4.194:5.1 | S: And what is the factor of trying to be pure in view? | T: “And what, TigerPaws, is the factor for exertion with regard to purity of view?
an4.194:5.2 | S: Take a mendicant who truly understands: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’. | T: There is the case where a monk discerns, as it has come to be, that ‘This is stress… This the origination of stress… This is the cessation of stress… This is the path of practice leading to the cessation of stress.’
an4.194:5.3 | S: This is called purity of view. | T: This is called purity of view.
an4.194:5.4 | S: They think: ‘I will fulfill such purity of view, or, if it’s already fulfilled, I’ll support it in every situation by wisdom.’ Their enthusiasm for that—their effort, zeal, vigor, perseverance, mindfulness, and situational awareness—is called the factor of trying to be pure in view. | T: (The thought,) ‘I will make complete this sort of purity of view when it is not yet complete, or I will protect it here & there with discernment when it is complete’: Any desire, effort, diligence, endeavor, relentlessness, mindfulness, & alertness there is called the factor for exertion with regard to purity of view.
an4.194:6.1 | S: And what is the factor of trying to be pure in freedom? | T: “And what, TigerPaws, is the factor for exertion with regard to purity of release?
an4.194:6.2 | S: That noble disciple—who has these factors of trying to be pure in ethics, mind, and view—detaches their mind from things that arouse greed, and frees their mind from things that it should be freed from. | T: That same noble disciple—endowed with this factor for exertion with regard to purity of virtue, this factor for exertion with regard to purity of mind, and this factor for exertion with regard to purity of view—makes his mind dispassionate with regard to phenomena that are conducive to passion, and liberates his mind with regard to phenomena that are conducive to liberation.
an4.194:6.3 | S: Doing so, they experience perfect freedom. | T: He—having made his mind dispassionate with regard to phenomena that are conducive to passion, and having liberated his mind with regard to phenomena that are conducive to liberation—touches right release.
an4.194:6.4 | S: This is called purity of freedom. | T: This is called purity of release.
an4.194:6.5 | S: They think: ‘I will fulfill such purity of freedom, or, if it’s already fulfilled, I’ll support it in every situation by wisdom.’ Their enthusiasm for that—their effort, zeal, vigor, perseverance, mindfulness, and situational awareness—is called the factor of trying to be pure in freedom. | T: (The thought,) ‘I will make complete this sort of purity of release when it is not yet complete, or I will protect it here & there with discernment when it is complete’: Any desire, effort, diligence, endeavor, relentlessness, mindfulness, & alertness there is called the factor for exertion with regard to purity of release.
an4.194:7.1 | S: These four factors of trying to be pure have been rightly explained by the Blessed One, who knows and sees, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha. They are in order to purify sentient beings, to get past sorrow and crying, to make an end of pain and sadness, to discover the system, and to realize extinguishment.” | T: “These, TigerPaws, are the four factors for exertion with regard to purity that have been rightly expounded by the Blessed One who knows & sees—the Worthy One, the Rightly Self-awakened One—for the purification of beings, for the overcoming of sorrow & lamentation, for the disappearance of pain & distress, for the attainment of the right method, & for the realization of unbinding.”
an4.194:7.2 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.1
an4.1:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
an4.1:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Vajjis at Wares Village. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying among the Vajjians at Bhaṇḍa Village.
an4.1:1.3 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: There he addressed the monks,
an4.1:1.4 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Monks!”
an4.1:1.5 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Yes, lord,” the monks responded to him.
an4.1:1.6 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said:
an4.1:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, due to not understanding and not penetrating four things, both you and I have wandered and transmigrated for such a very long time. | T: “It’s because of not understanding and not penetrating four things that we have transmigrated & wandered on for such a long, long time, you & I.
an4.1:2.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.1:2.3 | S: Noble ethics, | T: “It’s because of not understanding and not penetrating noble virtue that we have transmigrated & wandered on for such a long, long time, you & I.
an4.1:2.4 | S: immersion, | T: “It’s because of not understanding and not penetrating noble concentration that we have transmigrated & wandered on for such a long, long time, you & I.
an4.1:2.5 | S: wisdom, | T: “It’s because of not understanding and not penetrating noble discernment that we have transmigrated & wandered on for such a long, long time, you & I.
an4.1:2.6 | S: and freedom. | T: “It’s because of not understanding and not penetrating noble release that we have transmigrated & wandered on for such a long, long time, you & I.
an4.1:2.7 | S: These noble ethics, immersion, wisdom, and freedom have been understood and comprehended. Craving for continued existence has been cut off; the leash to existence is ended; now there’ll be no more future lives.” | T: “But now that noble virtue is understood & penetrated, noble concentration… noble discernment… noble release is understood & penetrated, craving for becoming is destroyed, the guide to becoming [craving] is ended, there is now no further becoming.”
an4.1:3.1 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: That is what the Blessed One said.
an4.1:3.2 | S: Then the Holy One, the Teacher, went on to say: | T: When the One Well-Gone had said that, he—the Teacher—said further:
an4.1:4.1 | S: “Ethics, immersion, and wisdom, | T: Unexcelled virtue, concentration, discernment,
an4.1:4.2 | S: and the supreme freedom: | T: & release
an4.1:4.3 | S: these things have been understood | T: have been understood
an4.1:4.4 | S: by Gotama the renowned. | T: by Gotama of glorious stature.
an4.1:5.1 | S: And so the Buddha, having insight, | T: Having known them directly,
an4.1:5.2 | S: explained this teaching to the mendicants. | T: he taught the Dhamma to the monks— he, the Awakened One
an4.1:5.3 | S: The teacher made an end of suffering, | T: the Teacher who has put an end to suffering & stress,
an4.1:5.4 | S: seeing clearly, he is fully quenched.” | T: the One with vision totally unbound.
an4.1:5.5 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.201
an4.201:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you an untrue person and an even more untrue person, | T: “Monks, I will teach you the person of no integrity, the person of less integrity than the person of no integrity,
an4.201:1.2 | S: a true person and an even truer person. | T: the person of integrity, & the person of more integrity than the person of integrity.
an4.201:1.3 | S: Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: Listen & pay careful attention. I will speak.”
an4.201:1.4 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “As you say, lord,” the monks responded to the Blessed One.
an4.201:1.5 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said,
an4.201:2.1 | S: “And what is an untrue person? | T: “And which, monks, is the person of no integrity?
an4.201:2.2 | S: It’s someone who kills living creatures, steals, commits sexual misconduct, lies, and consumes intoxicants of beer, wine, and liquor. | T: There is the case where a certain individual is one who takes life, takes what is not given [steals], engages in sexual misconduct, tells lies, and takes distilled & fermented drinks that cause heedlessness.
an4.201:2.3 | S: This is called an untrue person. | T: This is called a person of no integrity.
an4.201:3.1 | S: And what is an even more untrue person? | T: “And which is the person of less integrity than the person of no integrity?
an4.201:3.2 | S: It’s someone who kills living creatures, steals, commits sexual misconduct, lies, and consumes intoxicants of beer, wine, and liquor. And they encourage others to do these things. | T: There is the case where a certain individual himself is one who takes life and he encourages another in undertaking the taking of life.
an4.201:3.3 | P: attanā ca adinnādāyī hoti, parañca adinnādāne samādapeti; | T: He himself is one who takes what is not given and he encourages another in undertaking the taking of what is not given.
an4.201:3.4 | P: attanā ca kāmesumicchācārī hoti, parañca kāmesumicchācāre samādapeti; | T: He himself is one who engages in sexual misconduct and he encourages another in undertaking sexual misconduct.
an4.201:3.5 | P: attanā ca musāvādī hoti, parañca musāvāde samādapeti; | T: He himself is one who tells lies and he encourages another in undertaking the telling of lies.
an4.201:3.6 | P: attanā ca surāmerayamajjapamādaṭṭhāyī hoti, parañca surāmerayamajjapamādaṭṭhāne samādapeti. | T: He himself is one who takes distilled & fermented drinks that cause heedlessness and he encourages another in undertaking the taking of distilled & fermented drinks that cause heedlessness.
an4.201:3.7 | S: This is called an even more untrue person. | T: This is called a person of less integrity than the person of no integrity.
an4.201:4.1 | S: And what is a true person? | T: “And which is the person of integrity?
an4.201:4.2 | S: It’s someone who doesn’t kill living creatures, steal, commit sexual misconduct, lie, or consume intoxicants of beer, wine, and liquor. | T: There is the case where a certain individual abstains from taking life, abstains from taking what is not given, abstains from sexual misconduct, abstains from telling lies, and abstains from taking distilled & fermented drinks that cause heedlessness.
an4.201:4.3 | S: This is called a true person. | T: This is called a person of integrity.

