## an4.201
an4.201:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you an untrue person and an even more untrue person, | T: “Monks, I will teach you the person of no integrity, the person of less integrity than the person of no integrity,
an4.201:1.2 | S: a true person and an even truer person. | T: the person of integrity, & the person of more integrity than the person of integrity.
an4.201:1.3 | S: Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: Listen & pay careful attention. I will speak.”
an4.201:1.4 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “As you say, lord,” the monks responded to the Blessed One.
an4.201:1.5 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said,
an4.201:2.1 | S: “And what is an untrue person? | T: “And which, monks, is the person of no integrity?
an4.201:2.2 | S: It’s someone who kills living creatures, steals, commits sexual misconduct, lies, and consumes intoxicants of beer, wine, and liquor. | T: There is the case where a certain individual is one who takes life, takes what is not given [steals], engages in sexual misconduct, tells lies, and takes distilled & fermented drinks that cause heedlessness.
an4.201:2.3 | S: This is called an untrue person. | T: This is called a person of no integrity.
an4.201:3.1 | S: And what is an even more untrue person? | T: “And which is the person of less integrity than the person of no integrity?
an4.201:3.2 | S: It’s someone who kills living creatures, steals, commits sexual misconduct, lies, and consumes intoxicants of beer, wine, and liquor. And they encourage others to do these things. | T: There is the case where a certain individual himself is one who takes life and he encourages another in undertaking the taking of life.
an4.201:3.3 | P: attanā ca adinnādāyī hoti, parañca adinnādāne samādapeti; | T: He himself is one who takes what is not given and he encourages another in undertaking the taking of what is not given.
an4.201:3.4 | P: attanā ca kāmesumicchācārī hoti, parañca kāmesumicchācāre samādapeti; | T: He himself is one who engages in sexual misconduct and he encourages another in undertaking sexual misconduct.
an4.201:3.5 | P: attanā ca musāvādī hoti, parañca musāvāde samādapeti; | T: He himself is one who tells lies and he encourages another in undertaking the telling of lies.
an4.201:3.6 | P: attanā ca surāmerayamajjapamādaṭṭhāyī hoti, parañca surāmerayamajjapamādaṭṭhāne samādapeti. | T: He himself is one who takes distilled & fermented drinks that cause heedlessness and he encourages another in undertaking the taking of distilled & fermented drinks that cause heedlessness.
an4.201:3.7 | S: This is called an even more untrue person. | T: This is called a person of less integrity than the person of no integrity.
an4.201:4.1 | S: And what is a true person? | T: “And which is the person of integrity?
an4.201:4.2 | S: It’s someone who doesn’t kill living creatures, steal, commit sexual misconduct, lie, or consume intoxicants of beer, wine, and liquor. | T: There is the case where a certain individual abstains from taking life, abstains from taking what is not given, abstains from sexual misconduct, abstains from telling lies, and abstains from taking distilled & fermented drinks that cause heedlessness.
an4.201:4.3 | S: This is called a true person. | T: This is called a person of integrity.
an4.201:5.1 | S: And what is an even truer person? | T: “And which is the person of more integrity than the person of integrity?
an4.201:5.2 | S: It’s someone who doesn’t kill living creatures, steal, commit sexual misconduct, lie, or consume intoxicants of beer, wine, and liquor. And they encourage others to refrain from these things. | T: There is the case where a certain individual himself abstains from taking life and he encourages another in undertaking abstinence from taking life.
an4.201:5.3 | P: attanā ca adinnādānā paṭivirato hoti, parañca adinnādānā veramaṇiyā samādapeti; | T: He himself abstains from taking what is not given and he encourages another in undertaking abstinence from taking what is not given.
an4.201:5.4 | P: attanā ca kāmesumicchācārā paṭivirato hoti, parañca kāmesumicchācārā veramaṇiyā samādapeti; | T: He himself abstains from sexual misconduct and he encourages another in undertaking abstinence from sexual misconduct.
an4.201:5.5 | P: attanā ca musāvādā paṭivirato hoti, parañca musāvādā veramaṇiyā samādapeti; | T: He himself abstains from telling lies and he encourages another in undertaking abstinence from telling lies.
an4.201:5.6 | P: attanā ca surāmerayamajjapamādaṭṭhānā paṭivirato hoti, parañca surāmerayamajjapamādaṭṭhānā veramaṇiyā samādapeti. | T: He himself abstains from taking distilled & fermented drinks that cause heedlessness and he encourages another in undertaking abstinence from taking distilled & fermented drinks that cause heedlessness.
an4.201:5.7 | S: This is called an even truer person.” | T: This is called a person of more integrity than the person of integrity.”
an4.201:5.8 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.233
an4.233:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I declare these four kinds of deeds, having realized them with my own insight. | T: “Monks, these four types of kamma have been directly known, verified, & made known by me.
an4.233:1.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.233:1.3 | S: There are dark deeds with dark results; | T: There is kamma that is dark with dark result.
an4.233:1.4 | S: bright deeds with bright results; | T: There is kamma that is bright with bright result.
an4.233:1.5 | S: dark and bright deeds with dark and bright results; and | T: There is kamma that is dark & bright with dark & bright result.
an4.233:1.6 | S: neither dark nor bright deeds with neither dark nor bright results, which lead to the ending of deeds. | T: There is kamma that is neither dark nor bright with neither dark nor bright result, leading to the ending of kamma.
an4.233:2.1 | S: And what are dark deeds with dark results? | T: “And what is kamma that is dark with dark result?
an4.233:2.2 | S: It’s when someone makes hurtful choices by way of body, speech, and mind. | T: There is the case where a certain person fabricates an injurious bodily fabrication, fabricates an injurious verbal fabrication, fabricates an injurious mental fabrication.
an4.233:2.3 | S: Having made these choices, they’re reborn in a hurtful world, | T: Having fabricated an injurious bodily fabrication, having fabricated an injurious verbal fabrication, having fabricated an injurious mental fabrication, he rearises in an injurious world.
an4.233:2.4 | S: where hurtful contacts strike them. | T: On rearising in an injurious world, there he is touched by injurious contacts.
an4.233:2.5 | S: Touched by hurtful contacts, they experience hurtful feelings that are absolutely painful—like the beings in hell. | T: Touched by injurious contacts, he experiences feelings that are exclusively painful, like those of the beings in hell.
an4.233:2.6 | S: These are called dark deeds with dark results. | T: This is called kamma that is dark with dark result.
an4.233:3.1 | S: And what are bright deeds with bright results? | T: “And what is kamma that is bright with bright result?
an4.233:3.2 | S: It’s when someone makes pleasing choices by way of body, speech, and mind. | T: There is the case where a certain person fabricates a non-injurious bodily fabrication… a non-injurious verbal fabrication… a non-injurious mental fabrication.…
an4.233:3.3 | S: Having made these choices, they’re reborn in a pleasing world, | T: He rearises in a non-injurious world.…
an4.233:3.4 | S: where pleasing contacts strike them. | T: There he is touched by non-injurious contacts.…
an4.233:3.5 | S: Touched by pleasing contacts, they experience pleasing feelings of absolute happiness—like the gods of universal beauty. | T: He experiences feelings that are exclusively pleasant, like those of the Beautiful Dark Devas.
an4.233:3.6 | S: These are called bright deeds with bright results. | T: This is called kamma that is bright with bright result.
an4.233:4.1 | S: And what are dark and bright deeds with dark and bright results? | T: “And what is kamma that is dark & bright with dark & bright result?
an4.233:4.2 | S: It’s when someone makes both hurtful and pleasing choices by way of body, speech, and mind. | T: There is the case where a certain person fabricates a bodily fabrication that is injurious & non-injurious… a verbal fabrication that is injurious & non-injurious… a mental fabrication that is injurious & non-injurious.…
an4.233:4.3 | S: Having made these choices, they are reborn in a world that is both hurtful and pleasing, | T: He rearises in an injurious & non-injurious world.…
an4.233:4.4 | S: where hurtful and pleasing contacts strike them. | T: There he is touched by injurious & non-injurious contacts.…
an4.233:4.5 | S: Touched by both hurtful and pleasing contacts, they experience both hurtful and pleasing feelings that are a mixture of pleasure and pain—like humans, some gods, and some beings in the underworld. | T: He experiences injurious & non-injurious feelings, pleasure mingled with pain, like those of human beings, some devas, and some beings in the lower realms.
an4.233:4.6 | S: These are called dark and bright deeds with dark and bright results. | T: This is called kamma that is dark & bright with dark & bright result.
an4.233:5.1 | S: And what are neither dark nor bright deeds with neither dark nor bright results, which lead to the ending of deeds? | T: “And what is kamma that is neither dark nor bright with neither dark nor bright result, leading to the ending of kamma?
an4.233:5.2 | S: It’s the intention to give up dark deeds with dark results, bright deeds with bright results, and both dark and bright deeds with both dark and bright results. | T: The intention right there to abandon this kamma that is dark with dark result, the intention right there to abandon this kamma that is bright with bright result, the intention right there to abandon this kamma that is dark & bright with dark & bright result.
an4.233:5.3 | S: These are called neither dark nor bright deeds with neither dark nor bright results, which lead to the ending of deeds. | T: This is called kamma that is neither dark nor bright with neither dark nor bright result, leading to the ending of kamma.
an4.233:5.4 | S: These are the four kinds of deeds that I declare, having realized them with my own insight.” | T: “These, monks, are the four types of kamma directly known, verified, & made known by me.”
an4.233:5.5 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.235
an4.235:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I declare these four kinds of deeds, having realized them with my own insight. | T: “Monks, these four types of kamma have been directly known, verified, & made known by me.
an4.235:1.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
