## an4.179
an4.179:1.1 | S: Then Venerable Ānanda went up to Venerable Sāriputta, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Ven. Ānanda went to Ven. Sāriputta and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
an4.179:1.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, Ānanda sat down to one side, and said to Sāriputta: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to Ven. Sāriputta,
an4.179:1.3 | S: “What is the cause, Reverend Sāriputta, what is the reason why some sentient beings are not fully extinguished in this very life?” | T: “Friend Sāriputta, what is the cause, what is the reason, why some beings do not become totally unbound in the present life?”
an4.179:2.1 | S: “Reverend Ānanda, it’s because some sentient beings don’t really understand which perceptions make things worse, which keep things steady, which lead to distinction, and which lead to penetration. | T: “There’s the case, friend Ānanda, where beings do not discern, as it has come to be, that ‘This perception has a share in decline;’ ‘This perception has a share in stability;’ ‘This perception has a share in distinction;’ ‘This perception has a share in penetration.’
an4.179:2.2 | S: That’s the cause, that’s the reason why some sentient beings are not fully extinguished in this very life.” | T: This is the cause, this is the reason, why some beings do not become totally unbound in the present life.”
an4.179:3.1 | S: “What is the cause, Reverend Sāriputta, what is the reason why some sentient beings are fully extinguished in this very life?” | T: “And what, friend Sāriputta, is the cause, what is the reason, why some beings do become totally unbound in the present life?”
an4.179:3.2 | S: “Reverend Ānanda, it’s because some sentient beings truly understand which perceptions make things worse, which keep things steady, which lead to distinction, and which lead to penetration. | T: “There’s the case, friend Ānanda, where beings discern, as it has come to be, that ‘This perception has a share in decline;’ ‘This perception has a share in stability;’ ‘This perception has a share in distinction;’ ‘This perception has a share in penetration.’
an4.179:3.3 | S: That’s the cause, that’s the reason why some sentient beings are fully extinguished in this very life.” | T: This is the cause, this is the reason, why some beings become totally unbound in the present life.”
an4.179:3.4 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.181
an4.181:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, a warrior with four factors is worthy of a king, fit to serve a king, and is reckoned a factor of kingship. | T: “Endowed with four qualities, monks, a warrior is worthy of a king, an asset to a king, and counts as a very limb of his king.
an4.181:1.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.181:1.3 | S: He’s skilled in the basics, a long-distance shooter, a marksman, one who shatters large objects. | T: “There is the case where a warrior is skilled in his stance, able to shoot far, able to fire shots in rapid succession, and able to pierce great objects.
an4.181:1.4 | S: A warrior with these four factors is worthy of a king, fit to serve a king, and is reckoned a factor of kingship. | T: A warrior endowed with these four qualities is worthy of a king, an asset to a king, and counts as a very limb of his king.
an4.181:1.5 | S: In the same way, a mendicant with four qualities is worthy of offerings dedicated to the gods, worthy of hospitality, worthy of a religious donation, worthy of veneration with cupped palms, and is the supreme field of merit for the world. | T: “In the same way a monk endowed with four qualities is deserving of gifts, deserving of hospitality, deserving of offerings, deserving of respect, an unexcelled field of merit for the world.
an4.181:1.6 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.181:1.7 | S: He’s skilled in the basics, a long-distance shooter, a marksman, one who shatters large objects. | T: “There is the case where a monk is skilled in his stance, able to shoot far, able to fire shots in rapid succession, and able to pierce great objects. A monk endowed with these four qualities is deserving of gifts, deserving of hospitality, deserving of offerings, deserving of respect, an unexcelled field of merit for the world.
an4.181:2.1 | S: And how is a mendicant skilled in the basics? | T: “And how is a monk skilled in his stance?
an4.181:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant is ethical, restrained in the monastic code, conducting themselves well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, they keep the rules they’ve undertaken. | T: There is the case where a monk is virtuous. He dwells restrained in accordance with the Pāṭimokkha, consummate in his behavior & sphere of activity. He trains himself, having undertaken the training rules, seeing danger in the slightest faults.
an4.181:2.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant is skilled in the basics. | T: This is how a monk is skilled in his stance.
an4.181:3.1 | S: And how is a mendicant a long-distance shooter? | T: “And how is a monk one who is able to shoot far?
an4.181:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant truly sees any kind of form at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> form—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: There is the case where a monk sees any form whatsoever that is past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle; common or sublime; far or near—every form—as it has come to be with right discernment as: ‘This is not mine. This is not my self. This is not what I am.’
an4.181:3.3 | S: They truly see any kind of feeling … | T: “He sees any feeling whatsoever.…
an4.181:3.4 | S: perception … | T: “He sees any perception whatsoever.…
an4.181:3.5 | S: choices … | T: “He sees any fabrications whatsoever.…
an4.181:3.6 | S: consciousness at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> consciousness—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: “He sees any consciousness whatsoever that is past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle, common or sublime, far or near—every consciousness—as it has come to be with right discernment as: ‘This is not mine. This is not my self. This is not what I am.’
an4.181:3.7 | S: That’s how a mendicant is a long-distance shooter. | T: “This is how a monk is one who is able to shoot far.
an4.181:4.1 | S: And how is a mendicant a marksman? | T: “And how is a monk one who is able to fire shots in rapid succession?
an4.181:4.2 | S: It’s when they truly understand: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’. | T: There is the case where a monk discerns, as it has come to be, that ‘This is stress’ … ‘This is the origination of stress’ … ‘This is the cessation of stress’ … ‘This is the path of practice leading to the cessation of stress.’
an4.181:4.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant is a marksman. | T: This is how a monk is one who is able to fire shots in rapid succession.
an4.181:5.1 | S: And how does a mendicant shatter large objects? | T: “And how is a monk one who is able to pierce great objects?
an4.181:5.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant shatters the great mass of ignorance. | T: There is the case where a monk pierces right through the great mass of ignorance.
an4.181:5.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant shatters large objects. | T: This is how a monk is one who is able to pierce great objects right through.
an4.181:5.4 | S: A mendicant with these four qualities … is the supreme field of merit for the world.” | T: “Endowed with these four qualities, a monk is deserving of gifts, deserving of hospitality, deserving of offerings, deserving of respect, an unexcelled field of merit for the world.”
an4.181:5.5 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.182
an4.182:1.1 | S: “There are four things that no one can guarantee— | T: “For four things, monks, there is no guarantor—
an4.182:1.2 | S: not an ascetic, a brahmin, a god, a Māra, a divinity, or anyone in the world. | T: neither a contemplative, nor a brahman, nor a deva, nor a Māra, nor a Brahmā, nor anyone at all in the world.
an4.182:2.1 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.182:2.2 | S: No one can guarantee that someone liable to old age will not grow old. | T: “‘May what is subject to aging not age’: For this there is no guarantor—
an4.182:2.3 | P: samaṇo vā brāhmaṇo vā devo vā māro vā brahmā vā koci vā lokasmiṁ; | T: neither a contemplative, nor a brahman, nor a deva, nor a Māra, nor a Brahmā, nor anyone at all in the world.
an4.182:2.4 | S: No one can guarantee that someone liable to sickness will not get sick. | T: “‘May what is subject to illness not fall ill’: For this there is no guarantor—
an4.182:2.5 | P: samaṇo vā brāhmaṇo vā devo vā māro vā brahmā vā koci vā lokasmiṁ; | T: neither a contemplative, nor a brahman, nor a deva, nor a Māra, nor a Brahmā, nor anyone at all in the world.
an4.182:2.6 | S: No one can guarantee that someone liable to death will not die. | T: “‘May what is subject to death not die’: For this there is no guarantor—
an4.182:2.7 | P: samaṇo vā brāhmaṇo vā devo vā māro vā brahmā vā koci vā lokasmiṁ; | T: neither a contemplative, nor a brahman, nor a deva, nor a Māra, nor a Brahmā, nor anyone at all in the world.
an4.182:2.8 | S: No one can guarantee that the bad deeds done in past lives—corrupting, leading to future lives, hurtful, resulting in suffering and future rebirth, old age, and death—will not produce their result. | T: “‘May the results of evil actions—defiled, leading to further becoming, distressing, ripening in pain & future birth, aging, & death—not appear’: For this there is no guarantor—
an4.182:2.9 | P: samaṇo vā brāhmaṇo vā devo vā māro vā brahmā vā koci vā lokasmiṁ. | T: neither a contemplative, nor a brahman, nor a deva, nor a Māra, nor a Brahmā, nor anyone at all in the world.
an4.182:3.1 | S: These are the four things that no one can guarantee— | T: “For these four things, monks, there is no guarantor—
an4.182:3.2 | S: not an ascetic, a brahmin, a god, a Māra, a divinity, or anyone in the world.” | T: neither a contemplative, nor a brahman, nor a deva, nor a Māra, nor a Brahmā, nor anyone at all in the world.”
an4.182:3.3 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.192
an4.192:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, these four things can be known in four situations. | T: “Monks, these four traits may be known by means of four (other) traits.
an4.192:1.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.192:1.3 | S: You can get to know a person’s ethics by living with them. But only after a long time, not casually; only when attentive, not when inattentive; and only by the wise, not the witless. | T: “It’s through living together that a person’s virtue may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning.
an4.192:1.4 | S: You can get to know a person’s purity by dealing with them. … | T: “It’s through dealing with a person that his purity may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning.
an4.192:1.5 | S: You can get to know a person’s resilience in times of trouble. … | T: “It’s through adversity that a person’s endurance may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning.
an4.192:1.6 | S: You can get to know a person’s wisdom by discussion. But only after a long time, not casually; only when attentive, not when inattentive; and only by the wise, not the witless. | T: “It’s through discussion that a person’s discernment may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning.
an4.192:2.1 | S: ‘You can get to know a person’s ethics by living with them. But only after a long time, not casually; only when attentive, not when inattentive; and only by the wise, not the witless.’ | T: [1] “‘It’s through living together that a person’s virtue may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning’:
an4.192:2.2 | S: That’s what I said, but why did I say it? | T: Thus it was said. And in reference to what was it said?
an4.192:2.3 | S: Take an individual who’s living with someone else. They come to know: | T: “There is the case where one individual, through living with another, knows this:
an4.192:2.4 | S: ‘For a long time this venerable’s deeds have been broken, tainted, spotty, and marred. Their deeds and behavior are inconsistent. | T: ‘For a long time this person has been torn, broken, spotted, splattered in his actions. He hasn’t been consistent in his actions. He hasn’t practiced consistently with regard to the precepts.
an4.192:2.5 | S: This venerable is unethical, not ethical.’ | T: He is an unprincipled person, not a virtuous, principled one.’
an4.192:3.1 | S: Take another individual who’s living with someone else. They come to know: | T: And then there is the case where one individual, through living with another, knows this:
an4.192:3.2 | S: ‘For a long time this venerable’s deeds have been intact, impeccable, spotless, and unmarred. Their deeds and behavior are consistent. | T: ‘For a long time this person has been untorn, unbroken, unspotted, unsplattered in his actions. He has been consistent in his actions. He has practiced consistently with regard to the precepts.
an4.192:3.3 | S: This venerable is ethical, not unethical.’ | T: He is a virtuous, principled person, not an unprincipled one.’
an4.192:3.4 | S: That’s why I said that you can get to know a person’s ethics by living with them. But only after a long time, not a short time; only when attentive, not when inattentive; and only by the wise, not the witless. | T: “‘It’s through living together that a person’s virtue may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning’: Thus it was said. And in reference to this was it said.
an4.192:4.1 | S: ‘You can get to know a person’s purity by dealing with them. …’ | T: [2] “‘It’s through dealing with a person that his purity may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning’:
an4.192:4.2 | S: That’s what I said, but why did I say it? | T: Thus it was said. And in reference to what was it said?
an4.192:4.3 | S: Take an individual who has dealings with someone else. They come to know: | T: “There is the case where one individual, through dealing with another, knows this:
an4.192:4.4 | S: ‘This venerable deals with one person in one way. Then they deal with two, three, or many people each in different ways. | T: ‘This person deals one way when one-on-one, another way when with two, another way when with three, another way when with many.
an4.192:4.5 | S: They’re not consistent from one deal to the next. | T: His earlier dealings do not jibe with his later dealings.
an4.192:4.6 | S: This venerable’s dealings are impure, not pure.’ | T: He is impure in his dealings, not pure.’
an4.192:5.1 | S: Take another individual who has dealings with someone else. They come to know: | T: And then there is the case where one individual, through dealing with another, knows this:
an4.192:5.2 | S: ‘This venerable deals with one person in one way. Then they deal with two, three, or many people each in the same way. | T: ‘The way this person deals when one-on-one, is the same way he deals when with two, when with three, when with many.
an4.192:5.3 | S: They’re consistent from one deal to the next. | T: His earlier dealings jibe with his later dealings.
an4.192:5.4 | S: This venerable’s dealings are pure, not impure.’ | T: He is pure in his dealings, not impure.’
an4.192:5.5 | S: That’s why I said that you can get to know a person’s purity by dealing with them. … | T: “‘It’s through dealing with a person that his purity may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning’: Thus it was said. And in reference to this was it said.
an4.192:6.1 | S: ‘You can get to know a person’s resilience in times of trouble. …’ | T: [3] “‘It’s through adversity that a person’s endurance may be known, and then only after a long period, not a short period; by one who is attentive, not by one who is inattentive; by one who is discerning, not by one who is not discerning’:
an4.192:6.2 | S: That’s what I said, but why did I say it? | T: Thus it was said. And in reference to what was it said?
an4.192:6.3 | S: Take someone who experiences loss of family, wealth, or health. But they don’t reflect: | T: “There is the case where a person, suffering loss of relatives, loss of wealth, or loss through disease, doesn’t reflect:
an4.192:6.4 | S: ‘Worldly life is like that. Incarnation is like that. That’s why the eight worldly conditions revolve around the world, and the world revolves around the eight worldly conditions: | T: ‘That’s how it is when living together in the world. That’s how it is when gaining a personal identity [atta-bhāva, literally “self-state”]. When there is living in the world, when there is the gaining of a personal identity, these eight worldly conditions spin after the world, and the world spins after these eight worldly conditions:
an4.192:6.5 | S: gain and loss, fame and disgrace, blame and praise, pleasure and pain.’ | T: gain, loss, status, disgrace, censure, praise, pleasure, & pain.’
an4.192:6.6 | S: They sorrow and wail and lament, beating their breast and falling into confusion. | T: Suffering loss of relatives, loss of wealth, or loss through disease, he sorrows, grieves, & laments, beats his breast, becomes distraught.
an4.192:7.1 | S: Take someone else who experiences loss of family, wealth, or health. But they reflect: | T: And then there is the case where a person, suffering loss of relatives, loss of wealth, or loss through disease, reflects:
an4.192:7.2 | S: ‘Worldly life is like that. Incarnation is like that. That’s why the eight worldly conditions revolve around the world, and the world revolves around the eight worldly conditions: | T: ‘That’s how it is when living together in the world. That’s how it is when gaining a personal identity. When there is living in the world, when there is the gaining of a personal identity, these eight worldly conditions spin after the world, and the world spins after these eight worldly conditions:

